SELECT '873d73a2-d9bb-74f8-bc73-33fe503b4e7c', 'b590f544-ee2e-7348-91c8-612f44da4150', 'List', 'List', 'A list is Python''s dynamic array: an ordered, index-addressable, resizable collection (the same idea as C++ std::vector, Java ArrayList and the JavaScript Array). Arrays are the foundation that almost every other data structure is built on, so knowing exactly what is cheap and what is expensive on one is the first DSA skill.
Array — a block of elements stored in contiguous memory, so element i lives at base + i × size. That one formula is why reading arr[i] costs O(1) for any i.
Dynamic array (list) — an array that grows on demand. It keeps some spare capacity; when that runs out it allocates a bigger block, copies everything over and frees the old one. Python lists may also mix element types ([2, ''p'', True]) because each slot stores a reference to an object, not the object itself.
list   = [ 10,  20,  30,  40,  50 ]
index      0    1    2    3    4
-ve index -5   -4   -3   -2   -1
How a dynamic array grows
The list pre-allocates a little extra room. append writes into that room in O(1). Only when the room is gone does the list resize:
Allocate a bigger block
Typically a multiple of the current capacity (×2 in most textbooks and in C++ vector; CPython uses a gentler ≈1.125× plus a constant, Java ArrayList uses 1.5×).
Copy
Move all n existing elements into the new block — this is the O(n) step.
Free the old block
The list now points at the new memory and has spare capacity again.
capacity 4 (full)        append(50) → grow to capacity 8, copy 4, write 1
┌────┬────┬────┬────┐    ┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐
│ 10 │ 20 │ 30 │ 40 │ →  │ 10 │ 20 │ 30 │ 40 │ 50 │    │    │    │
└────┴────┴────┴────┘    └────┴────┴────┴────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘
                                                ↑ next 3 appends are O(1)
Why append is O(1) amortised
Append 1 000 numbers to an empty list that starts with room for 10. Resizes happen at 10, 20, 40, 80, 160, 320, 640 elements — 7 copies totalling about 1 270 element moves, i.e. roughly one extra move per append. The occasional O(n) copy is spread over the O(n) cheap appends that follow it, so each append costs O(1) amortised.
With doubling, inserting n elements costs at most n + (1 + 2 + 4 + … + n) < 3n element writes → O(1) amortised per append
	Python	C++	Java	JavaScript
Type	list	std::vector<T>	ArrayList<T>	Array
Fixed-size cousin	array.array, NumPy	T arr[n], std::array	int[] arr	typed arrays
Mixed element types	yes (references)	no	no (one T)	yes
Negative indices	yes	no	no	arr.at(-1) only
Random access
arr[i] is one multiplication and one memory read — O(1) for any index.
Cache friendly
Elements sit next to each other, so iterating pulls whole cache lines of useful data (locality of reference).
Slow in the middle
Inserting or deleting at index i shifts every element after it — O(n). Searching an unsorted array is also O(n).
Operations and their complexities
Operation	Python	Time	Why
Read / write by index	l[i], l[i] = x	O(1)	address arithmetic
Append at end	l.append(x)	O(1) amortised	spare capacity; rare copy
Pop from end	l.pop()	O(1)	nothing to shift
Insert at index	l.insert(i, x)	O(n)	shifts n - i elements right
Delete by index / value	del l[i], l.pop(i), l.remove(x)	O(n)	shifts elements left (remove also searches)
Search	x in l, l.index(x), l.count(x)	O(n)	linear scan
Length	len(l)	O(1)	stored with the list
Min / max / sum	min(l), max(l), sum(l)	O(n)	one pass
Reverse	l.reverse(), l[::-1]	O(n)	swap from both ends
Sort	l.sort(), sorted(l)	O(n log n)	Timsort
Slice	l[a:b]	O(b − a)	copies the slice
Pitfall
l.insert(0, x) and l.pop(0) look innocent but shift the whole list every time — a loop of them is O(n²). Use collections.deque when you need cheap operations at the front.
Indexing and negative indices
Python lets you count from the back with negative indices; the other languages need n - k. Reading past either end is an IndexError in Python, an exception in Java, undefined in JavaScript and undefined behaviour in C++ (v.at(i) checks, v[i] does not).
l = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
print("first:", l[0])
print("fourth:", l[3])
print("last:", l[-1])             # negative index counts from the end
print("third from end:", l[-3])
print("length:", len(l))
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> v = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int n = v.size();
    cout << "first: " << v[0] << "\n";
    cout << "fourth: " << v[3] << "\n";
    cout << "last: " << v[n - 1] << "\n";            // no negative indices: use n - k
    cout << "third from end: " << v[n - 3] << "\n";
    cout << "length: " << n << "\n";
}
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> l = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50));
        int n = l.size();
        System.out.println("first: " + l.get(0));
        System.out.println("fourth: " + l.get(3));
        System.out.println("last: " + l.get(n - 1));            // no negative indices: use n - k
        System.out.println("third from end: " + l.get(n - 3));
        System.out.println("length: " + n);
    }
}
