SELECT 'f0f1e2f2-284e-73ee-9e0b-da9b80417c16', 'ef5f28c4-d41e-7bce-b343-fc9732e0b293', 'Recursion', 'Recursion', 'Recursion is a function solving a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem. It is the natural shape of divide and conquer, tree and graph traversal, backtracking and dynamic programming — and the source of the classic bugs: a missing base case and a blown call stack.
Recursion — a technique where a function calls itself, directly or indirectly, until it reaches a base case that can be answered without a further call. Each call works on a strictly smaller input so that the chain of calls is guaranteed to end.
Kind	Shape	Example
Direct recursion	f calls f	fact(n) calls fact(n − 1)
Indirect (mutual) recursion	f calls g, g calls f	is_even(n) calls is_odd(n − 1), which calls is_even(n − 2)
Not recursion	a loop repeats a block without a new call	for i in range(n): print("GFG") — the original notes'' first example is this, a plain loop
Recursion background: how calls nest
A function call does not finish until the function it called has returned. The program below shows the order in which lines run when fun2 calls fun1: the before lines go in, the after lines come out in reverse. Recursion is exactly this picture with the same function on every level.
def fun1():
    print("Function 1 is called")

def fun2():
    print("Before Function 1")
    fun1()                       # fun2 pauses here until fun1 returns
    print("After Function 1")

print("Before Function 2")
fun2()
print("After Function 2")
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun1() {
    cout << "Function 1 is called\n";
}

void fun2() {
    cout << "Before Function 1\n";
    fun1();                      // fun2 pauses here until fun1 returns
    cout << "After Function 1\n";
}

int main() {
    cout << "Before Function 2\n";
    fun2();
    cout << "After Function 2\n";
}
public class Main {
    static void fun1() {
        System.out.println("Function 1 is called");
    }

    static void fun2() {
        System.out.println("Before Function 1");
        fun1();                  // fun2 pauses here until fun1 returns
        System.out.println("After Function 1");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before Function 2");
        fun2();
        System.out.println("After Function 2");
    }
}
